# /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/zz-pidp1-mods.rules
#
# Which device nodes an unprivileged member of the pidp1 group may open to
# drive the PiDP-1 front panel, and nothing else: this is what replaces
# "chmod +s .../newpanel" (src/panel_pidp1/setpriv.sh), so no panel driver is
# installed setuid root.
#
# The gpiochip line is the primary path: newpanel's libgpiod backend drives
# direction, output values, input levels and pulls through the kernel GPIO
# character device. Fedora already hands gpiochip* to the "gpio" group
# (60-gpiochip.rules / 90-gpio.rules), but "gpio" is every chip on the
# machine - a laptop has a dozen, most of them PMIC blocks whose lines are
# power rails - and re-tagging another package's group is not ours to
# decide. So, exactly as pidp10's 99-pidp10.rules does, only the controller
# the panel is actually wired to is re-tagged here, into the cabinet's
# operator group (sysusers.d fragment pidp1-mods.conf): the 40-pin header
# lines are on the SoC GPIO controller on a Pi 4 and earlier
# (pinctrl-bcm2835) and on the RP1 I/O hub on a Pi 5 (pinctrl-rp1), which is
# a separate chip and needs its own match. Everywhere else no rule applies,
# the nodes keep the mode the distribution chose, and newpanel says "no
# panel" - the honest answer. Unusual wiring (a GPIO expander) needs a rule
# of its own (udevadm info -a -n /dev/gpiochipN, then a drop-in here) plus
# PIDP1_GPIO_CHIP=... PIDP1_PANEL_FORCE=1 to tell the driver which chip.
#
# The name is deliberately zz-*, and not by vanity. udev applies whole rule
# files in lexicographic order and a node has exactly one group, so on a
# shared host the LAST rule that re-tags the header's chip owns it - and the
# sibling cabinets ship rules for the very same controller (pidp10's
# 99-pidp10.rules is why gpiochip4 comes up root:pidp10 once that package is
# installed, even with ours in place). The panel is wired to pidp1 here, so
# ours must sort after theirs, and no 99-pidp1* spelling achieves that:
# udev's version-aware ordering puts "99-pidp1" before "99-pidp10" and
# "99-pidp11" alike, which is why pidp11 got ahead of pidp10 by digit
# accident. zz-* sorts after every numbered rule Fedora or any of us ships.
#
# The gpiomem line serves the register backend - the historical path, still
# used for pin functions libgpiod cannot express and still needed where no
# gpiochip matched above (Raspberry Pi OS ships gpiomem; Fedora's kernel
# does not). Like gpiochip*, it cannot over-reach: a machine that is not a
# Pi has no gpiomem*, so the rule simply never applies elsewhere.
#
# The /dev/mem fallback in Pinctrl/gpiolib.c stays root-only on purpose:
# opening /dev/mem to a group is a machine-wide security hole, not panel
# access. A box with neither path working runs setpriv.sh instead; that is
# the choice for it, not a rule here.
#
#  udev(7), systemd-udevd(8), libgpiod(7)
#  udevadm control --reload && udevadm trigger -c add /dev/gpiochip* /dev/gpiomem*

KERNEL=="gpiochip*", SUBSYSTEM=="gpio", \
    DRIVERS=="pinctrl-bcm2835|pinctrl-rp1", GROUP="pidp1", MODE="0660"

KERNEL=="gpiomem[0-9]*", SUBSYSTEM=="misc", GROUP="pidp1", MODE="0660"
